From 88accd8ea378f291b1f65b7d3ac43b34c1987e9f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adrien Delmas <adrien@adriendelmas.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 00:07:30 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 087/148] hydra: render passes - lights, Karma LPEs,
 cryptomatte, shader AOVs

The delegate only knew five passes and their Houdini spellings; any other render
variable fell with a warning. A variable is now resolved, in this order:
- by its name: C, N, P, Pz, the Cycles names (diffuse_direct, ao, mist, uv,
  motion...) and those of Karma (direct_diffuse, indirect_reflect, Ce...);
- by its source (sourceName/sourceType of the aovSettings): a raw Cycles or
  Karma name (ray:hitPz, ray:hitN...), one of the light path expressions that
  Karma's settings write (C<RD>.*L, C<RG>L, CV.*L...), the st primvar;
- otherwise it is a shader AOV, under the name of its source.

Cycles divides its light passes by the surface color (Blender's convention);
Karma keeps them multiplied. Karma's names and its LPEs are remultiplied at read
time by the color pass: a diffuse sphere reads 0.752 like the beauty, or 0.94
under the Cycles name. Only one pass per type: two of the same type under two
names stay separated by the film, the kernel writes only one and the other - and
what is computed from it - came out black.

Cryptomatte: CryptoObject/Material/AssetNN enable the layers, all created in the
order the kernel expects. Objects and materials now carry a name (the path of
the prim, of its parent for the asset, of the material): without it everything
hashed to the same identifier.

Shader AOVs: Hydra only keeps the nodes that a terminal reaches, an isolated
aov_output never arrived. It receives in the registry a surface input and a
closure output that lets it through, to be plugged between a shader and the
material output like Karma's AOVs; the terminal knows how to follow an output
carried by a helper node.

tools/bench_aovs.py, on the repo side, verifies the seven cases.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
